 ],
 "literal": "With strength hard to conquer ... / ... kuruntu, jasmine, saying / other than these four, there are no flowers; / black-stalked kodo millet, dark-eared foxtail millet, / small-vined horse gram, along with spot-shining field beans, / other than these four, there is no food; / tudi-drummer, bard, parai-drummer, kadamban, saying / other than these four, there are no clans; / standing in front of hostile enemies and blocking them, / as [he] fell, having slain the elephant with shining, loftily bearing tusks, / except for worshipping his hero stone, / there are no gods to be worshipped by scattering paddy.",
 "translation": "In this land of strength hard to conquer, there are no flowers other than kuruntu and jasmine. There is no food other than black-stalked kodo millet, dark-eared foxtail millet, small-vined horse gram, and spot-shining field beans. There are no clans other than the tudi-drummer, the bard, the parai-drummer, and the kadamban. And there are no gods to be worshipped by scattering paddy, except for the hero stone of the warrior who stood in front of hostile enemies, blocked them, and fell after slaying their elephant with shining, loftily bearing tusks.",
 "modern": "வெல்லுதற்கரிய வலிமையையுடைய இந்நிலத்தில், குருந்தும் முல்லையும் ஆகிய இவற்றைத் தவிர வேறு பூக்கள் இல்லை. கரிய தாளையுடைய வரகு, கரிய கதிரையுடைய தினை, சிறிய கொடியையுடைய கொள், புள்ளிகள் விளங்கும் அவரை ஆகிய இந்நான்கைத் தவிர வேறு உணவும் இல்லை. துடியன், பாணன், பறையன், கடம்பன் ஆகிய இந்நான்கு குடியினரைத் தவிர வேறு குடிகளும் இல்லை. பகைவர்களை எதிர்த்து நின்று தடுத்து, விளங்கும் ஏந்திய தந்தங்களையுடைய யானையைக் கொன்று வீழ்ந்த வீரனுடைய நடுகல்லை வணங்குவதே அல்லாமல், நெல்லைத் தூவி வழிபடும் வேறு தெய்வங்களும் இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "The poet Manguṭi Kiḻār describes the heroic and simple life of the Mullai (forest/pastoral) region, belonging to the Puram theme of Vākai (victory) and the sub-theme of Mūtiṉ Mullai (the ancient warrior house). He asserts that in this land of fierce strength, there are only four types of flowers (including kuruntu and jasmine), four types of food crops (kodo millet, foxtail millet, horse gram, and field beans), and four ancient clans (tudi-drummers, bards, parai-drummers, and the kadamban clan). Most importantly, he declares that there is no god to be worshipped with offerings of rice other than the hero stone (naṭukal) raised for the brave warrior who died in battle after slaying an enemy elephant. This poem beautifully captures the martial ethos of early Tamil society, where the ultimate object of reverence was the fallen hero who protected his community. Key words include 'kallē' (the hero stone) and 'kaṭavuḷ' (god).",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
